{"name":"Ivy","hex":"#277b74","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Ivy","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A unisex given name from English.\nA female given name from English; popular in the UK in the beginning of the 20th century.\n\n A male given name from English.","A female given name from English; popular in the UK in the beginning of the 20th century."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A university that is part of the Ivy League."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Ivy"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hedera","blocks":[{"text":"Hedera, commonly called ivy, is a genus of 12–15 species of evergreen climbing or ground-creeping woody plants in the family Araliaceae, native to Western Europe, Central Europe, Southern Europe, Macaronesia, northwestern Africa and across central-southern Asia east to Japan and Taiwan. Several species are cultivated as climbing ornamentals, and the name ivy especially denotes common ivy, known in North America as \"English ivy\", which is frequently planted to clothe brick walls."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Hedera_algeriensis_kz01.jpg/330px-Hedera_algeriensis_kz01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hedera","wikidata":"Q26771"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Ivy_fruits.JPG/500px-Ivy_fruits.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Ivy_fruits.JPG/1280px-Ivy_fruits.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Ivy_fruits.JPG/500px-Ivy_fruits.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hedera helix adult leaves and unripe berries in Ayrshire, Scotland","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIvy_fruits.JPG"},{